Rem Capability: file
Rem ==================================================================
Rem file.shoddy — Shoddy standard library: text files, line-oriented
Rem Built on the eight whole-file builtins, each documented one by one
Rem immediately below: ReadFile TryReadFile WriteFile TryWriteFile
Rem AppendFile FileExists DeleteFile TryDeleteFile — plus str.shoddy.
Rem Normalizes \r\n on read.
Rem File words are effectful, like Print/Input: keep them at the edges.
Rem ==================================================================

Include "str.shoddy"

Rem ---- the builtins this machine is built on -------------------------
Rem
Rem NOT Defs. The engine dispatches these, and a Def whose name is a
Rem builtin is refused; they are written here because file.shoddy is
Rem where a reader goes looking for them. The flat reference is section
Rem 10 of docs/spec.html, and the stack effects below are the ones the
Rem compiler's own Effects.cs table declares.
Rem
Rem NO HANDLES: a text file is read or written WHOLE. Paths resolve
Rem against the working directory. Everything here is effectful.
Rem
Rem THE Try* WORDS ARE NOT DUPLICATES, and the reason is the whole
Rem shape of error handling in this language. Shoddy has no catchable
Rem errors, so an aborting word cannot be asked "would this work?" —
Rem the asking IS the doing, and a failure ends the run. The Try* forms
Rem are how a program survives a path it did not choose, and FileExists
Rem cannot stand in for any of them: a directory reports False, an
Rem existing file that cannot be opened reports True, and a file that
Rem is there when you ask can be locked by the time the delete lands.

Rem ReadFile(path As String) As String                ( path -- s )
Rem     The whole file as one string, newlines and all. Aborts if the
Rem     path cannot be read. ReadLines below is the one that splits it.

Rem TryReadFile(path As String) As Result             ( path -- result )
Rem     The same read with the failure REPORTED rather than fatal:
Rem     Ok(text), or Err(why, 0) where why is CANNOT READ 'path' (…)
Rem     ending in one of NO SUCH FILE, IS A DIRECTORY, ACCESS DENIED or
Rem     UNREADABLE. That phrase is a closed set of Shoddy's own and
Rem     never the host platform's exception text, which varies by OS and
Rem     locale. At is 0 — a failure to open a file has no position IN
Rem     it. It answers a Result rather than a Boolean because, unlike
Rem     TryWriteFile, it has a payload to carry on success.

Rem WriteFile(path As String, s As String)            ( path s -- )
Rem     Write the string to path, replacing whatever was there. Aborts
Rem     on failure.

Rem TryWriteFile(path As String, s As String) As Boolean
Rem                                                   ( path s -- ok )
Rem     The same write, answering True on success rather than aborting.
Rem     A Boolean and not a Result: a write has nothing to hand back.

Rem AppendFile(path As String, s As String)           ( path s -- )
Rem     Add the string to the end of path, creating the file if it is
Rem     not there. Aborts on failure, and has no guarded twin.

Rem FileExists(path As String) As Boolean             ( path -- flag )
Rem     Whether there is a readable file at path. A directory answers
Rem     False. This is NOT a pre-flight for the aborting words — see the
Rem     note above.

Rem DeleteFile(path As String)                        ( path -- )
Rem     Delete the file at path. A missing file aborts.

Rem TryDeleteFile(path As String) As Boolean          ( path -- ok )
Rem     The same delete, answering True if the file went and False if it
Rem     was not there or could not be removed.

Rem ---- this machine's own words --------------------------------------

Def ReadLines(path As String) As List Of String
    Let c = Replace(ReadFile(path), "\r", "")
    If c = "" Then
        { }
    Else
        If EndsWith(c, "\n") Then
            Split(Left(c, Len(c) - 1), "\n")
        Else
            Split(c, "\n")

Def WriteLines(path As String, xs As List Of String)
    WriteFile(path, Join(xs, "\n") & "\n")

Def AppendLine(path As String, s As String)
    AppendFile(path, s & "\n")
